/**

\mainpage catlib User Manual

Introduction
------------

The catlib library (*libcat.a*) is a collection of C++ classes which
are common to the CTkit applcations and generally play a supporting
role. This documentation is provided in the hope that some of these
may find more general utility.

Library Components
------------------

The three main components of the library are:

- DistributedRunManager
- InputParameters
- XCATCompressor

### DistributedRunManager ###


The distributed run manager (i.e., DRM) is a singleton class which
distributes a parallel application to multiple process and/or multiple
hosts. The DRM uses a client/server model and cooperatively elects a
server to maintain the state of the distributed process. An article is
provided (<a href="../articles/DRM.pdf">here</a> ) to
explain the design of the tool.

This tool is primarily encoded in four C++ classes in the library. They are:

- `DistributedRunManager`
- `ElectionManager`
- `Ticket`
- `TicketManager`

A detailed example is include in the library's `test/DRM` folder.

### InputParameters ###

The InputParameters feature of the library is a tool for translating a
parameter file into values which can be used in an application. The
parameter file is formatted, per line, as:

tag: value

InputParameters allows an application designer to define tag names and
value formats for easy storage and retrieval.

This tool is primarily encoded in four C++ classes in the library. They are:

- `InputParams`
- `ParamItem`
- `ParamTemplates`

A detailed example is include in the library's `test/InputParams` folder.

### XCATPhantomCompressor ###

The XCATPhantomCompressor tool is designed to compress a large file of
repeating integers in such a way that they may be extracted without
decompressing the entire file. It is a two stage process.

First, the file is compressed using Run Length Encoding (RLE). This
technique simply counts the number of repeating values until a new
value is encountered. The start location, end location and the value
are stored in a `tree_key` struct.

The Second step is to create a B-tree of these structs to improve
search time from O(n) to O(log(n)). Additionally, there is no
requirement to ''de-compress'' anything. This technique is perfectly
suited for compressing phantoms for use in memory on GPU platforms.

This tool is completely encoded with the single class,
`XCATPhantomCompress`. A detailed example is include in the library's
`test/XCATPhantomCompress` folder.

*/